From: TheGammaSqueeze (GammaOS), azahar-nano; dArkOS RG52 Mini port
Subject: The right eye is told apart by framebuffer; with stereo_skip the rasterizer cuts it

GammaOSNextDistribution-14 e62a0042457 (2026-10-25), the core's part:

The right eye disabler guessed the second eye from timing: the first top screen
list after the left transfer was the right eye. Castlevania: Mirror of Fate renders
each eye into its own target and transfers both at the end of its frame, so the
rule cut a left eye list every other frame; the intro flickered as the wording
came in and the cutscenes alternated between a frame with the characters and one
without. The disabler now learns which target feeds which framebuffer from the
transfers. A transfer into the right framebuffer is cut whatever the target
pattern. When the eyes have distinct targets the lists are rendered; targets that
swap eyes between frames render everything too. A title without a right
framebuffer skips nothing, and titles with a shared target keep the timing rule.

dArkOS: when GammaOS's stereo_skip is on (patches 013 and 014: Castlevania at
Auto), the transfer into the right framebuffer goes on to the rasterizer instead,
which learns the right eye's target from it, cuts it itself and leaves that
target's draws out. Cut here, the rasterizer never learned the target and
Castlevania drew both eyes again (its intro 9 fps instead of 21).

